{#
  Template: ai_summary.md.j2
  Description: Generates an AI-written table summary using the prompt(...) helper.

  Requirements:
    - nao_config.yaml must contain an `llm` section with provider + credentials.
    - `db.templates` must include `ai_summary` for this template to render.

  Optional context, passed by `nao sync` when the files exist:
    - project_rules: the project RULES.md, without group-restricted blocks.
    - table_annotations: the table annotations.md, without its front matter.
#}
{% set columns = db.columns() %}
{% set table_description = db.description() %}
{% set profiling = profiling if profiling is defined else db.profiling() %}
{% set has_profiling = profiling and profiling.columns %}
{% set computed_at = computed_at if computed_at is defined else (profiling.computed_at if has_profiling else "") %}
{% set preview_rows = db.preview(limit=10) %}
{% set instruction -%}
You are generating short data documentation for analysts.
Write a concise markdown summary for table {{ dataset }}.{{ table_name }}.

Include:
1. What this table appears to represent.
{% if has_profiling %}
2. Data-quality and distribution notes grounded only in the profiling statistics.
{% else %}
Skip data-quality and distribution claims entirely because profiling statistics are unavailable. Do not guess.
{% endif %}
3. Suggested analytical use cases.

Table description:
{{ table_description if table_description else "No description provided." }}

Columns:
{% for col in columns %}
- {{ col.name }} ({{ col.type }}){% if col.description %}: {{ col.description }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% if project_rules or table_annotations %}

Project rules and table annotations:
The analytics agent reads the rules and annotations below alongside this summary, and they are authoritative. Your summary must stay consistent with them:
- Never contradict them.
- Never recommend a column, join, filter or calculation that they forbid or describe as unreliable.
- If the profiling statistics or the preview seem to contradict them, follow them; you may point out the discrepancy.
- Do not restate them in full, since the agent already has them.
- Do not invent business definitions (acronyms, statuses, metrics) that neither they nor the table description provide.
{% if project_rules %}

Project rules (RULES.md):
<project_rules>
{{ project_rules }}
</project_rules>
{% endif %}
{% if table_annotations %}

Table annotations (annotations.md):
<table_annotations>
{{ table_annotations }}
</table_annotations>
{% endif %}

{% endif %}
Preview (a tiny, non-representative sample of up to 10 rows — do NOT infer data quality, null-rates, volume, or value distributions from these rows):
{{ preview_rows | to_json(indent=2) }}

{% if has_profiling %}
Profiling statistics (JSON):
Use these statistics for data-quality and distribution observations, including nulls, distinct counts, min/max, and top values.
{% if profiling.scope %}
{% set scope = profiling.scope %}
Profiling scope: these statistics cover only the {{ scope.profiled_row_count }} rows matching the partition filter `` {{ scope.partition_filter }} ``, not the full table{% if scope.table_row_count is not none %} ({{ scope.table_row_count }} rows){% endif %}. Every column's total_count, null_percentage, distinct_count, min/max, mean and top values describe that subset only. Say so in the data-quality notes, and never compare these statistics against the full-table row count (for example to infer uniqueness or fan-out).
{% endif %}
{{ profiling | to_json(indent=2) }}
{% endif %}
{%- endset %}
{% set summary = prompt(instruction) %}
# {{ table_name }} - AI Summary

**Dataset:** `{{ dataset }}`

**Computed at:** `{{ computed_at }}`

{{ summary }}
